Claims:
[
    "A **golden dataset** is a high-quality, curated collection of inputs (questions, prompts) and their corresponding *correct* outputs (answers, responses) used as a benchmark for evaluating LLMs.",
    "The goal of a golden dataset is to create a dataset where the 'correct' answers are as reliable and accurate as possible, ensuring trustworthy evaluation results when testing an LLM against it.",
    "Thumbs-up and thumbs-down signals act as **real-time user feedback**—a form of implicit evaluation—where users provide proxy indicators of whether the model’s response was correct or incorrect.",
    "When users give thumbs-down to an LLM’s response, it is treated as a strong hint that the model’s response was likely **wrong or unsatisfactory** for that input.",
    "Conversely, thumbs-up signals suggest the model’s answer **aligned with the user’s expectations** and may be considered correct.",
    "Thumbs-up/down signals can be **mined** to identify pairs of (input, output) where user feedback aligns with correctness, adding them to the golden dataset as 'positive' or 'negative' examples.",
    "Thumbs-down responses could be added as **negative examples** (incorrect outputs) in the golden dataset, while thumbs-up responses could be added as **positive examples** (correct outputs).",
    "This process is a form of **active learning** or **user-driven annotation**, where the dataset is refined based on actual user behavior rather than predefined labels.",
    "Online evaluations often rely on collecting **real-time interaction data** as users interact with the model, with thumbs-up/down signals being one way to capture this dynamically.",
    "Storing thumbs-up/down signals alongside other metrics (like latency, token usage, or errors) allows later analysis of **patterns**—such as clusters of thumbs-down responses—to identify recurring issues in the model’s performance.",
    "These patterns can inform the creation of the golden dataset by highlighting **questions where the model frequently fails**.",
    "The method of using thumbs-up/down signals is described as **tricky** because it relies on **user perception of correctness**, which isn’t always objective.",
    "A user might thumb down not because the answer was **wrong**, but because they disliked the **tone or style** of the response.",
    "When aggregated across many interactions, thumbs-up/down signals can still reveal **statistical trends** in the model’s performance, even if individual signals are subjective.",
    "The key is to use thumbs-up/down signals as a **starting point** for further validation, such as having human annotators review flagged examples to confirm correctness.",
    "Other signals (e.g., users requesting human intervention or providing escalation details) are also forms of **behavioral feedback** that can indirectly signal dissatisfaction with the model’s output.",
    "While thumbs-up/down are **explicit** signals, other feedback types are **implicit** but equally valuable for identifying problematic interactions.",
    "Thumbs-up and thumbs-down signals help build a golden dataset by providing a **scalable, user-driven way** to identify and label examples of correct and incorrect model outputs.",
    "The method is **not perfect** because user feedback isn’t always reliable, but it offers a **practical way to gather data in real-world settings** and refine the dataset over time.",
    "The next step in this process would likely involve **curating or augmenting** the dataset by having human reviewers validate flagged examples before finalizing the golden standard.",
    "The material mentions that the *how* of online evaluations (e.g., technical implementation) has not yet been covered in detail."
