    "Here’s how it works: you casually interact with the system by asking a small set of questions (typically 5–10) and judge its performance based on how it feels rather than structured metrics.",
    "If the answers seem 'right' or 'satisfactory' in that moment, you might conclude the app is working well.",
    "Key characteristics of vibe testing are:",
    "- Informal: No predefined criteria or rigorous methodology—just a gut check.",
    "- Subjective: Your personal judgment (e.g., 'This answer seemed correct') drives the evaluation.",
    "- Non-repeatable: The questions you ask today might not be the same tomorrow, so results aren’t consistent or reproducible.",
    "- Quick and low-effort: It’s a 'try it out and see' approach, often done before sharing the app with others or deploying it.",
    "The term mirrors vibe coding—a playful way to describe coding without discipline or testing—because vibe testing mirrors that same lack of structure.",
    "It’s the LLM equivalent of 'I built it, it worked for me, so it must be fine.'",
    "Why isn’t vibe testing enough for deploying an LLM app?",
    "Vibe testing doesn’t account for edge cases or real-world variability.",
    "Vibe testing relies on a tiny, self-selected sample of questions—often the ones that you think of or feel comfortable asking.",
    "In production, users might ask anything: ambiguous queries, adversarial inputs, or scenarios you never anticipated.",
    "For example, Air Canada’s chatbot failed spectacularly when asked about bereavement fares because the testers didn’t probe that specific edge case.",
    "Vibe testing misses these gaps entirely.",
    "Vibe testing is not scalable or reliable.",
    "The course emphasizes that vibe testing is not repeatable.",
    "If you test today and get 'good' results, there’s no guarantee the app will perform the same way in 10 days—or even tomorrow—because the questions change.",
    "Production systems need consistent performance, not a 'feels okay' pass/fail based on fleeting interactions.",
    "Vibe testing ignores systemic risks.",
    "LLMs can fail in ways that aren’t obvious from a few casual prompts:",
    "- Hallucinations: Generating confident but incorrect information (like Air Canada’s chatbot’s refund policy misstatement).",
    "- Bias or harmful outputs: Vibe testing might overlook toxic or discriminatory responses if they don’t surface in your test questions.",
    "- Performance degradation: Over time, models drift, or inputs evolve (e.g., new regulations, slang, or cultural shifts), but vibe testing doesn’t track these changes systematically.",
    "Vibe testing lacks measurable thresholds for 'good enough'.",
    "Vibe testing is qualitative: 'It felt right.'",
    "But production requires quantifiable benchmarks.",
